<h2>Respiratory Emergencies — Asthma &amp; COPD</h2>
<blockquote>"Respiratory failure ฆ่าได้เร็ว — ต้อง recognize + treat ทันที"</blockquote>
<h3>ภาพรวม</h3>
<p>Respiratory emergencies เป็นหัวข้อที่ EMT พบบ่อยมาก — ทั้ง asthma, COPD, pneumonia, pulmonary edema, anaphylaxis</p>
<p>สิ่งสำคัญ: <strong>Recognize respiratory failure early</strong> → อย่ารอจน patient exhausted</p>
<h3>Respiratory Assessment</h3>
<h4>Look, Listen, Feel</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Assessment</th><th>Normal</th><th>Abnormal</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Rate</strong></td><td>12-20/min</td><td>&lt;10 or &gt;30 = concerning</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Depth</strong></td><td>Tidal volume adequate</td><td>Shallow = fatigue</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Pattern</strong></td><td>Regular</td><td>Irregular = brainstem issue</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Breath sounds</strong></td><td>Clear bilateral</td><td>Wheezing, crackles, absent</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Effort</strong></td><td>Normal</td><td>Accessory muscles, tripod, nasale flaring</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Color</strong></td><td>Pink</td><td>Pale, cyanotic, mottled</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Mental status</strong></td><td>Alert</td><td>Agitated → Confused → Unresponsive (CO2 narcosis)</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Signs of Respiratory Distress</h4>
<ul><li>Tachypnea</li><li>Accessory muscle use (sternocleidomastoid, intercostals)</li><li>Tripod position (leaning forward)</li><li>Nasal flaring (infants)</li><li>Retractions (intercostal, subcostal, suprasternal)</li><li>Unable to speak full sentences</li></ul>
<h4>Signs of Respiratory Failure (⚠️ Critical)</h4>
<ul><li>Bradypnea (exhaustion)</li><li>Altered mental status (CO2 narcosis or hypoxia)</li><li>Cyanosis</li><li>Absent breath sounds</li><li>Paradoxical breathing</li><li>SpO2 &lt;90% despite O2</li></ul>
<h3>Asthma (หอบหืด)</h3>
<h4>Pathophysiology</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Bronchospasm</strong> (กล้ามเนื้อหดเกร็ง) + <strong>Airway inflammation</strong> + <strong>Mucus plugging</strong></li><li>→ Airflow obstruction → air trapping → hyperinflation</li></ul>
<h4>Severity Classification</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Severity</th><th>Symptoms</th><th>Peak Flow</th><th>SpO2</th><th>Action</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Mild</strong></td><td>Dyspnea with talking, speaks in sentences, mild wheezing</td><td>&gt;70% predicted</td><td>&gt;94%</td><td>Bronchodilator + monitor</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Moderate</strong></td><td>Dyspnea with talking, speaks in phrases, moderate wheezing</td><td>50-70%</td><td>90-94%</td><td>Bronchodilator + systemic steroids + consider CPAP</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Severe</strong></td><td>Dyspnea at rest, speaks in words, loud wheezing or silent chest</td><td>&lt;50%</td><td>&lt;90%</td><td>Aggressive bronchodilators + epinephrine + CPAP/intubation</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Near-fatal</strong></td><td>Silent exhaustion, bradycardia, altered mental status</td><td>Un recordable</td><td>&lt;88%</td><td><strong>INTUBATE</strong></td></tr>
</table>
<h4>⚠️ Silent Chest = DANGER!</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Silent chest</strong> ใน asthma = severe obstruction + exhaustion → near-fatal</li><li>ไม่มี wheezing ไม่ใช่ดี — อาจแย่มาก!</li></ul>
<h4>Prehospital Treatment</h4>
<p>1. <strong>High-flow O2</strong> — target SpO2 94-98%</p>
<p>2. <strong>Bronchodilator:</strong></p>
<ul><li><strong>Albuterol (Salbutamol)</strong> 2.5-5mg nebulized (or 4-8 puffs MDI + spacer)</li><li><strong>Ipratropium (Atrovent)</strong> 0.5mg nebulized (add to albuterol in severe)</li><li>Repeat albuterol every 20 minutes (up to 3 doses) if severe</li></ul>
<p>3. <strong>Systemic steroids:</strong> Methylprednisolone 125mg IV or Prednisolone 50mg PO</p>
<p>4. <strong>Epinephrine 0.3-0.5mg IM</strong> — if severe + not responding to bronchodilators</p>
<p>5. <strong>CPAP</strong> — if severe, conscious, not protecting airway</p>
<p>6. <strong>Magnesium sulfate 2g IV</strong> (ALS) — severe refractory asthma</p>
<p>7. <strong>Intubation</strong> — last resort (near-fatal, exhaustion)</p>
<h3>COPD (Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease)</h3>
<h4>Types</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Pathology</th><th>Classic Appearance</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Chronic Bronchitis</strong></td><td>Mucus hypersecretion, chronic cough</td><td>"Blue bloater" — cyanotic, obese, edematous</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Emphysema</strong></td><td>Alveolar destruction, air trapping</td><td>"Pink puffer" — thin, barrel-chested, pursed-lip breathing</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Pathophysiology</h4>
<ul><li>Chronic airflow limitation → air trapping → hyperinflation</li><li><strong>CO2 retainer</strong> — chronic hypercapnia → respiratory center becomes insensitive to CO2</li><li><strong>Hypoxic drive</strong> — relies on low O2 to stimulate breathing (controversial but important concept)</li></ul>
<h4>⚠️ O2 in COPD — Important Concept</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Traditional teaching:</strong> COPD patients are "CO2 retainers" → high O2 suppresses hypoxic drive → respiratory depression</li><li><strong>Current evidence:</strong> Hypoxia kills faster than hypercapnia → <strong>give O2 to target SpO2 88-92%</strong></li><li><strong>Practical approach:</strong> Start low (2-4 L/min nasal cannula) → titrate to SpO2 88-92% → if deteriorating → increase support</li></ul>
<h4>Prehospital Treatment</h4>
<p>1. <strong>O2 titrated</strong> — target SpO2 88-92% (start 2-4 L/min NC)</p>
<p>2. <strong>Bronchodilator:</strong> Albuterol 2.5mg + Ipratropium 0.5mg nebulized</p>
<p>3. <strong>CPAP</strong> — if respiratory distress (reduces work of breathing)</p>
<p>4. <strong>Steroids:</strong> Methylprednisolone 125mg IV</p>
<p>5. <strong>Treat underlying cause:</strong> Infection (antibodies in hospital), PE, etc.</p>
<p>6. <strong>Intubation</strong> — if respiratory failure despite treatment</p>
<h3>Other Respiratory Emergencies</h3>
<h4>Pulmonary Edema (Acute Heart Failure)</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Signs:</strong> Crackles (rales), pink frothy sputum, orthopnea, JVD, peripheral edema</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong></li><li><strong>High-flow O2</strong></li><li><strong>CPAP</strong> (very effective for pulmonary edema)</li><li><strong>Nitroglycerin</strong> 0.4mg SL (SBP &gt;100) — reduces preload</li><li><strong>Furosemide 40-80mg IV</strong> (ALS)</li><li><strong>Morphine 2-4mg IV</strong> (ALS) — reduces anxiety + preload</li></ul>
<h4>Pneumonia</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Signs:</strong> Fever, cough, crackles, consolidation on CXR</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> O2, IV fluids, antibiotics (hospital)</li></ul>
<h4>Pulmonary Embolism (PE)</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Signs:</strong> Sudden dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, hypoxia, DVT signs</li><li><strong>Risk factors:</strong> Immobilization, surgery, pregnancy, OCP, cancer, DVT</li><li><strong>Massive PE:</strong> Syncope, hypotension, cardiac arrest (PEA)</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> O2, IV fluids (careful), anticoagulation (hospital), thrombolytics (massive PE)</li></ul>
<h4>Pneumothorax</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Signs:</strong> Sudden dyspnea, absent breath sounds, hyperresonance</li><li><strong>Simple:</strong> O2, monitor, chest tube if large</li><li><strong>Tension:</strong> Needle decompression → chest tube</li></ul>
<h4>Anaphylaxis</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Signs:</strong> Urticaria, angioedema, bronchospasm, hypotension</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> <strong>Epinephrine 0.3-0.5mg IM</strong> (anterolateral thigh) — FIRST!</li><li>Repeat every 5-15 minutes</li><li>IV fluid bolus (1-2L)</li><li>Albuterol nebulizer (bronchospasm)</li><li>Diphenhydramine 50mg IV</li><li>Methylprednisolone 125mg IV</li><li>Glucagon 1-5mg IV (if on beta-blockers)</li></ul>
<h3>Airway Sounds Guide</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Sound</th><th>Location</th><th>Indicates</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Wheezing</strong></td><td>Expiratory</td><td>Lower airway obstruction (asthma, COPD)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Stridor</strong></td><td>Inspiratory</td><td>Upper airway obstruction (croup, epiglottitis, foreign body)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Crackles (Rales)</strong></td><td>Inspiratory</td><td>Fluid in alveoli (pneumonia, pulmonary edema)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Rhonchi</strong></td><td>Both</td><td>Secretions in large airways</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Grunting</strong></td><td>Expiratory</td><td>Infant respiratory distress (keeps alveoli open)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Silent chest</strong></td><td>—</td><td>Severe obstruction + exhaustion = near-fatal</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>Quiz</h3>
<p><strong>Q1:</strong> COPD patient — SpO2 target เท่าไหร่?</p>
<p><strong>A1:</strong> <strong>88-92%</strong> — titrate O2 to target, don't over-oxygenate but don't let them desaturate</p>
<p><strong>Q2:</strong> Asthma silent chest หมายถึงอะไร?</p>
<p><strong>A2:</strong> <strong>Near-fatal asthma</strong> — severe obstruction + exhaustion → imminent respiratory arrest</p>
<p><strong>Q3:</strong> Anaphylaxis first-line treatment?</p>
<p><strong>A3:</strong> <strong>Epinephrine 0.3-0.5mg IM</strong> (anterolateral thigh) — never delay epinephrine</p>
<h3>ภาษาไทย: Respiratory Emergencies สำหรับ EMT</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Silent chest = danger</strong> — imminent arrest</li><li><strong>COPD O2 target 88-92%</strong> — don't over-oxygenate</li><li><strong>Anaphylaxis = Epinephrine IM first</strong></li><li><strong>CPAP</strong> effective สำหรับ pulmonary edema + severe asthma/COPD</li><li><strong>Know your wheezes vs stridor vs crackles</strong></li></ul>
